DAY 10 — CREATE DATABASE, TABLE, DATA TYPES & INSERT
============================================================


1. CREATE DATABASE

Used to create a database.

CREATE DATABASE PlacementDB;


2. USE

Used to select the database we want to work with.

USE PlacementDB;


3. CREATE TABLE

Used to create a table.

CREATE TABLE STUDENT (
    ID INT,
    NAME VARCHAR(50),
    AGE INT,
    MARKS DECIMAL(5,2)
);


4. IMPORTANT DATA TYPES

INT
→ Stores whole numbers.

Example:

AGE INT;


VARCHAR(n)
→ Stores variable-length text.

Example:

NAME VARCHAR(50);


CHAR(n)
→ Stores fixed-length text.

Example:

CODE CHAR(5);


DECIMAL(p,s)
→ Stores precise decimal numbers.

Example:

SAL DECIMAL(10,2);


DATE
→ Stores date.

Example:

JOIN_DATE DATE;


DATETIME
→ Stores date + time.

Example:

CREATED_AT DATETIME;


5. CHAR vs VARCHAR

CHAR:
→ Fixed-length string

VARCHAR:
→ Variable-length string


6. DECIMAL(10,2)

DECIMAL(10,2) means:

10 = TOTAL number of digits
2  = digits after decimal point

Therefore:
8 digits can be before the decimal
2 digits can be after the decimal

Example:

12345678.90


7. INSERT INTO

Used to add new rows/data.

Example:

INSERT INTO STUDENT (ID, NAME, AGE, MARKS)
VALUES (101, 'RAM', 21, 87.50);


8. INSERT MULTIPLE ROWS

INSERT INTO STUDENT (ID, NAME, AGE, MARKS)
VALUES
(102, 'SHYAM', 22, 76.25),
(103, 'MOHAN', 20, 91.00),
(104, 'RAHUL', 21, 68.50);


9. INSERT NULL

If a column allows NULL:

INSERT INTO STUDENT (ID, NAME, AGE, MARKS)
VALUES (105, 'PRIYA', 20, NULL);


10. VERIFY DATA

SELECT *
FROM STUDENT;


Better practice:

Specify column names when inserting data.

Instead of:

INSERT INTO STUDENT
VALUES (...);

Prefer:

INSERT INTO STUDENT (ID, NAME, AGE, MARKS)
VALUES (...);


IMPORTANT:

CREATE DATABASE       → DDL
CREATE TABLE          → DDL
INSERT                → DML
SELECT                → DQL


