DAY 11 — UPDATE
============================================================

UPDATE is a DML command.

It is used to MODIFY EXISTING DATA.


1. BASIC UPDATE SYNTAX

UPDATE table_name
SET column_name = value
WHERE condition;


Example:

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = 5000
WHERE EMPNO = 101;


2. UPDATE MULTIPLE COLUMNS

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = 5500,
    DEPTNO = 20
WHERE EMPNO = 101;


3. WHY WHERE IS IMPORTANT

This is dangerous:

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = 5000;

Because there is no WHERE condition.

It can update the salary of EVERY employee.


Safer:

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = 5000
WHERE EMPNO = 101;

Only employee 101 is updated.


4. UPDATE USING CONDITIONS

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = 4000
WHERE DEPTNO = 20;

This updates all employees belonging to department 20.


5. UPDATE WITH MULTIPLE CONDITIONS

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = 6000
WHERE DEPTNO = 10
AND JOB = 'MANAGER';


6. UPDATE NULL VALUES

Correct:

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET COMM = 500
WHERE EMPNO = 101
AND COMM IS NULL;

Remember:

COMM IS NULL     → correct
COMM = NULL      → wrong


7. UPDATE BASED ON EXISTING VALUE

Increase salary by 10%:

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = SAL * 1.10;

This affects EVERY employee.

For only department 10:

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL = SAL * 1.10
WHERE DEPTNO = 10;


8. UPDATE USING CASE

UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET SAL =
    CASE
        WHEN DEPTNO = 10 THEN SAL * 1.10
        WHEN DEPTNO = 20 THEN SAL * 1.05
        ELSE SAL
    END;


Meaning:

Department 10 → 10% increase
Department 20 → 5% increase
Other departments → unchanged


9. VERIFY UPDATE

After updating, check the data:

SELECT *
FROM EMPLOYEE;

Or:

SELECT EMPNO, ENAME, SAL
FROM EMPLOYEE
WHERE EMPNO = 101;


10. INSERT vs UPDATE

INSERT:
→ Adds a NEW row.

UPDATE:
→ Modifies an EXISTING row.


